.TH CBMAP_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_create_full \- create a BST map with full customization
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap cbmap_create_full(ccol_data_type key_type,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        ccol_comparison_proc_t custom_comparison_proc,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a self-balancing BST map, with full control by the caller over the
key type, the memory management and the comparison function.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
key_type                 Type of the keys that the map stores
mmgmt_procs              Custom memory management, or NULL
custom_comparison_proc   Custom comparison function, or NULL
err                      Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new BST map on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Default comparison for ccol_char: the native char comparison of this
platform, which is signed or unsigned to match the behavior of char on
this platform
Default comparison for ccol_signed_char and for short/int/long/long_long:
a signed comparison, which does not depend on the char signedness of this
platform. Note that ccol_signed_char is a scalar signed char or its typedef
int8_t; a signed char * string key is ccol_string instead
Default comparison for the unsigned integer types and ccol_pointer: an
unsigned comparison
Default comparison for ccol_float/double/long_double: a numeric
floating-point comparison, in which a NaN key sorts as greater than every
non-NaN key and equal to every other NaN key
Default comparison for ccol_string: a strcmp-equivalent lexicographic order
For ccol_other_types (for example a struct key type) the default comparison
is a raw memcmp of the representation, which compares indeterminate padding
bytes and compares each pointer member by address, not by the object that
the pointer points at. Give custom_comparison_proc for a struct key
Every default comparison above applies to two keys of the same stored size.
Only a key type of variable width, that is ccol_string or ccol_other_types,
can have two keys of different sizes. For two such keys, the map compares
the common prefix first; if the common prefix is equal, the map compares
the sizes, and the shorter key comes first. Every key of a fixed-width key
type is exactly ccol_fixed_width_data_type_size(3) bytes wide, and every
entry point that takes a key_pair needs that exact width. This is also true
when the caller gives a custom_comparison_proc, because that function gets
two bare key pointers and no sizes, so it reads a width that the key type
fixes.
A custom comparison gets void* pointers to the key data, and a key that
comes in through the raw layer can be at any address. Read a key that is
wider than one byte through memcpy, not through a pointer cast to the key
type
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_create (3),
